# Playwright conventions

## Refusals (hard stops)

When a request asks for any of the following, refuse. Stop and raise the conflict.

- Refuse to weaken or delete an assertion so a test passes. Stop and raise the conflict.
- Refuse to heal a failure that triage classified as a real app bug. Stop and raise the conflict.
- Refuse to replace a role-based locator with CSS or XPath. Stop and raise the conflict.
- Refuse to use `waitForTimeout`. Stop and raise the conflict.
- Refuse to hardcode a credential. Stop and raise the conflict.
- Refuse to use the `any` type. Stop and raise the conflict.

## 1. Locators

Use `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` or `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId` only with a comment on why nothing higher worked, and disambiguate a multi-match with `.filter({ hasText })`.

✅ Good (`pages/NewProgramModal.ts`):

```typescript
this.dialog = page.getByRole('dialog').filter({
  has: page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'New Program' }),
});
this.programNameField = this.dialog.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Program Name' });
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
page.locator('.modal input').first();
page.locator('xpath=//button[1]');
```

**Why:** a CSS or XPath locator, or `.first()`, follows DOM order, so a second dialog or a restyle clicks the wrong control.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with a web-first assertion that retries: `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `.toBeEnabled()`, or `.toHaveText()`.

✅ Good (`tests/auth.setup.ts`):

```typescript
await expect(programsPage.heading).toBeVisible();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramButton).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad (`tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
const dialogVisible = await programsPage.newProgramModal.dialog.isVisible();
expect(dialogVisible).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** a fixed sleep races the clock, and a one-shot `isVisible()` reads the DOM before the dialog has painted.

## 3. Page Objects

Put every locator and user action on a page object in `pages/`; the spec states intent and holds every `expect(...)`.

✅ Good (`pages/LoginPage.ts`):

```typescript
async signIn(email: string, password: string) {
  await this.emailField.fill(email);
  await this.passwordField.fill(password);
  await this.signInButton.click();
}
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Programs' })).toBeVisible();
```

**Why:** an inline locator in the spec duplicates the page object, so a renamed button updates one file and leaves the other red.

## 4. Auth

Feature tests start from the `setup` project's `storageState` at `playwright/.auth/user.json`; a login-page test clears that state and signs in through `LoginPage`.

✅ Good (`tests/ds5-program-list-display.spec.ts`):

```typescript
test.use({ storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] } });
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('admin@didaxis.studio');
await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('hardcoded-password');
```

**Why:** a hardcoded password leaks into git, and a UI login inside a programs test fails when the form changes even though login is not what that test checks.

## 5. Test data

Happy-path values come from a Faker factory under `test-data/factories`. Invalid values live in a static `.ts` file, so a generated string cannot accidentally become valid. Secrets and URLs come from `process.env`. A path or message used in more than one file comes from an enum.

✅ Good (`test-data/factories/program.factory.ts`):

```typescript
import { faker } from '@faker-js/faker';

export type ProgramDraft = {
  name: string;
  description: string;
};

/** Happy-path program fields. The suffix keeps the name unique across workers. */
export function buildProgram(overrides: Partial<ProgramDraft> = {}): ProgramDraft {
  const suffix = faker.string.alphanumeric(8);
  return {
    name: overrides.name ?? `Web Development ${suffix}`,
    description: overrides.description ?? faker.lorem.sentence(),
  };
}
```

Curated invalid set (`test-data/programs.invalid.ts`):

```typescript
export const invalidProgramNames = {
  empty: '',
  whitespace: '   ',
  tooLong: 'A'.repeat(256),
} as const;
```

Repeated path, once a second file needs the same string:

```typescript
export enum ProgramPath {
  List = '/programs',
  Collection = '/api/programs',
}
```

```typescript
const token = process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN;
const baseURL = process.env.DIDAXIS_URL;
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
const programName = `Web Development ${Date.now()}`;
await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('hardcoded-password');
```

**Why:** a copied timestamp drifts between specs, a hardcoded password lands in git, and a Faker value meant to be invalid can come out valid.

### Cleanup

A test that creates a program imports `test` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts` and calls `trackProgram(uuid)` immediately; the fixture sends `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>` with `Authorization: Bearer` from `DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN`.

✅ Good (`tests/ds1-create-new-academic-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
import { test, expect } from '../fixtures/cleanup.fixture';

await createProgramWithTracking(page, programsPage, programName, description, trackProgram);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
import { test } from '@playwright/test';

test.afterAll(async () => {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: /Delete/ }).click();
});
```

**Why:** a hand-written `afterAll` skips cleanup when the test fails first, so the next run collides with a program it did not mean to keep.

## 6. API testing

Call the real programs API and assert the response; never fulfill the request the test is checking, and never hardcode the bearer token.

**Advised** — no Playwright `request` spec in the repo yet. Cleanup already deletes with `fetch` and `DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN` in `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`.

✅ Good:

```typescript
test('GET /api/programs returns 200', async ({ request }) => {
  const response = await request.get('/api/programs', {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` },
  });
  await expect(response).toBeOK();
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: '[]' }),
);
expect((await request.get('/api/programs')).status()).toBe(200);
```

**Why:** fulfilling the call under test only proves the mock ran, so a broken API stays green.

## 7. Network mocking

Fulfill only the programs call that forces a UI condition — POST save 503, GET list 200 with `{ data: [] }`, GET list 200 with a malformed body, plus 401, 403, 404, 501, 502, and 300 — one case per test, through existing page objects, asserting copy read from the live page.

✅ Good (`tests/support/programs-flow.helpers.ts`):

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() === 'GET') {
    await route.fulfill({
      status: 200,
      contentType: 'application/json',
      body: JSON.stringify({ data: [] }),
    });
    return;
  }
  await route.continue();
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 201, body: JSON.stringify({ data: { id: 'fake' } }) }),
);
await programsPage.createProgram(name, description);
await expect(programsPage.programRowByName(name)).toHaveCount(1);
```

**Why:** mocking the POST a persistence test is verifying never creates a row on the server, so the assertion checks the stub and `trackProgram` has nothing real to delete.

## 8. Accessibility

Keep one axe test and one keyboard test for the programs page: `new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a','wcag2aa']).analyze()` with `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])`, then tab to the primary control, `toBeFocused()`, Enter, and the dialog open, through page objects.

**Advised** — `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts` runs axe, but it records violations instead of failing the assertion below.

✅ Good:

```typescript
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await new AxeBuilder({ page }).disableRules(['color-contrast']).analyze();
```

**Why:** `.disableRules()` drops a real violation so the scan goes green while the page stays inaccessible.

## 9. Assertions

Assert from the spec with a retrying matcher, and leave the expected outcome the same when a locator or a wait changes.

✅ Good (`tests/ds1-create-new-academic-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await expect(programsPage.programRowByName(programName)).toHaveCount(1);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
expect(await programsPage.programRowByName(programName).count()).toBe(1);
```

**Why:** `count()` samples the DOM once, so the assertion fails when the new row arrives a moment later.

## 10. Anti-flake

Keep `fullyParallel: true`, `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`, `baseURL` from `process.env.DIDAXIS_URL`, `trace: 'on-first-retry'`, `locale: 'en-CA'`, and `timezoneId: 'America/Toronto'`, with the `setup` project and `storageState` dependency left in place; do not raise retries above 2 or set `workers: 1` in this config. `npm run test:destructive` is the only command that passes `--workers=1`. Freeze a relative timestamp with `page.clock.install()` before the page object navigates.

**Advised** — no spec installs a clock yet.

✅ Good:

```typescript
test('program row shows a frozen relative timestamp', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-10-30T15:00:00Z') });
  const programsPage = new ProgramsPage(page);
  await programsPage.goto();
  await expect(programsPage.updatedText('Web Development 2026')).toHaveText('2 hours ago');
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
retries: 5,
workers: 1,
await expect(page.getByText(/ago/)).toBeVisible();
```

**Why:** retries above 2 and `workers: 1` in this config hide a broken or order-dependent test, and an unfrozen "ago" string changes between a laptop and CI. Shared settings are the exception: they run only under `npm run test:destructive`.

## 11. Tags

Each `test()` carries exactly one of `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, `@e2e`, or `@destructive`. The tag sits on the test, never on `describe`.

| Tag | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `@smoke` | The one critical happy path of a feature |
| `@sanity` | The other happy-path checks in that feature |
| `@regression` | Negative cases, edge cases, mocked failures, and accessibility |
| `@api` | A test whose assertion is the HTTP API |
| `@e2e` | A browser journey that crosses features, such as creating a program and then adding a semester |
| `@destructive` | A test that changes locale, roles, flags, or settings |

`@destructive` is only that last case, and those tests run serially. A test that creates a program and deletes it with `trackProgram` keeps `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, or `@e2e`.

Any test that changes locale, roles, flags, or settings restores the previous value in `test.afterEach` or `test.afterAll`. Run that set with `npm run test:destructive` (`--workers=1`). A file with more than one of them also sets `test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' })`.

**Advised** — no spec changes locale, roles, flags, or settings yet.

✅ Good:

```typescript
test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' });

test.afterEach(async ({ page }) => {
  const settingsPage = new SettingsPage(page);
  await settingsPage.restoreLocale('en-CA');
});

test('French locale is shown in the shell', { tag: '@destructive' }, async ({ page }) => {
  const settingsPage = new SettingsPage(page);
  await settingsPage.switchLocale('fr-CA');
  await expect(settingsPage.heading).toHaveText('Programmes');
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
test.describe('programs', { tag: '@smoke' }, () => {
  test('create then delete a program this test made', { tag: ['@e2e', '@destructive'] }, async () => {
    await settingsPage.switchLocale('fr-CA');
  });
});
```

**Why:** a tag on `describe`, or two tags on one test, makes `--grep` run the wrong set. Deleting a program the test created is not a shared setting. Changing the locale without `afterEach` or `afterAll` leaves the next test in the wrong language.

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
| --- | --- |
| `getByRole` → `getByLabel` / `getByPlaceholder` → `getByText` → `getByTestId` (comment why) | CSS, XPath, or `.first()` |
| `.filter({ hasText })` when several elements match | A locator that matches more than one node |
| `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `.toBeEnabled()` / `.toHaveText()` | `waitForTimeout`, or `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` |
| Locators and actions in `pages/`; `expect` in `tests/` | Inline locators or assertions inside a page object |
| `setup` + `storageState` for feature tests; empty `storageState` for the login page | A hardcoded password, or a UI login in a test that is not about login |
| A Faker factory in `test-data/factories` for happy-path data; invalid values in a static `.ts` file; secrets and URLs from `process.env`; a repeated path or message in an enum | A hardcoded password, the same invalid string pasted into each spec, or Faker for a value that must stay invalid |
| `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts` and `trackProgram(uuid)` for a program the test created | A UI `afterAll` that deletes that program, or deleting a program the test did not create |
| Exactly one of `@smoke`, `@sanity`, `@regression`, `@api`, `@e2e`, `@destructive` on `test()`; `@destructive` restores locale, roles, flags, or settings in `afterEach` or `afterAll` | A tag on `describe`, two tags on one test, or `@destructive` on a test that only deletes its own program |
| Real API call, `Bearer` from `DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN`, `expect(response).toBeOK()` | Mocking the endpoint the test verifies, or a token in source |
| `page.route` to force 503, empty 200, malformed 200, 401, 403, 404, 501, 502, 300; copy from the live page | Invented error text, or `trackProgram` after a fulfilled write that persisted nothing |
| Axe `wcag2a` + `wcag2aa` with `toEqual([])`, and a separate keyboard test | `.disableRules()`, `.include()` / `.exclude()` without a comment, or two checks in one test |
| Retrying matchers; same assertion meaning when a locator changes | A one-shot `.count()` or `.isVisible()` boolean |
| `retries` 2 on CI and 0 locally; `en-CA` and `America/Toronto`; `page.clock.install()` for relative time; `--workers=1` only on `npm run test:destructive` | `workers: 1` in `playwright.config.ts`, retries above 2, or wall-clock "ago" text |
